#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd98d233-50ae-538f-9ab1-03b1028a79b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=\\frac{5}{6}t+\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358505} {:answer/id #uuid "7a341548-1213-5609-9861-3793f4607179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358506} {:answer/id #uuid "a528bb8b-4f94-5c25-b4b3-b722bb5f74ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=5+\\frac{5}{6}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358507} {:answer/id #uuid "a58e441a-b64b-5736-8941-bf99e46494f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=5+\\frac{5}{6}t+\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358508} {:answer/id #uuid "73d19e40-ba02-5c7b-a452-b6944ddfaffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=5+\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a341548-1213-5609-9861-3793f4607179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358506} :answer-field/id #uuid "41cf300e-79d9-5b26-9829-c540109921a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "470653e9-5d5b-542e-9020-2a9f1d9e16a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-103778" :question/problem "A gas station pump dispenses gas into an empty gas tank at a rate of $\\frac{11}{6}t$ liters per second. Write an expression for $A(t),$ the total volume of gas in the tank at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time measured in second." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=\\frac{11}{6}t$ be the rate at which the gas pump dispenses gas, and let $A(T)$ be the total number of liters in the car after $T$ seconds.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{11}{6}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{11}{6}\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{11{T}^{2}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nA(t)=\\frac{11{t}^{2}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3945f55a-2d0a-5515-801b-55af29af24d9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f95ca8be-e73e-5244-a320-018dd4061aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358512} {:answer/id #uuid "e7284f24-a13a-5561-8590-3d350cff79e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7284f24-a13a-5561-8590-3d350cff79e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358513} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c993e2-b41f-5743-b634-4b00f2295784" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e6fc43c-b4e2-588d-9575-ad3c07d6f70b" :question/math-academy-id "q-346534" :question/problem "Calculate $F(9)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F(9)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=9,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(9)={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(9) & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{9}{t}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{t}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =12({9}^{1/2}-{1}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =12(3-1) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd239d4-ae29-5444-86bc-e19f304be084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289467} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9ee719-ac45-5fcf-aba5-15ea999e1ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c9ee719-ac45-5fcf-aba5-15ea999e1ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289468} :answer-field/id #uuid "21ac806a-e453-5c07-8432-b0b5058d7f2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71c74918-41fb-5354-ad06-5491e1e76191" :question/math-academy-id "q-346576" :question/problem "The total number of pages of a document printed $t$ minutes after printing begins increases at a rate of $\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ If, at $t=0$ minutes, the printer had already produced $40$ pages, how many pages will the printer have produced after $2$ minutes?\n\nAfter $2$ minutes the printer has produced\n{{field-1}} pages." :question/worked-solution "Let $a(t)=\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}$ be the rate at which the number of pages produced is changing, and let $A(T)$ be the number of pages produced $T$ minutes after printing begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{18}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{t+1}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =40+(-\\frac{18}{T+1})-(-\\frac{18}{1}) \\\\  & =40-\\frac{18}{T+1}+18 \\\\  & =58-\\frac{18}{T+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of pages produced $T=2$ minutes after printing begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(2)=40-\\frac{18}{2+1}+18=52.\n$$\n\nThus, after $2$ minutes, the printer has produced $52$ pages."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "851dcf61-831e-5b7c-9f8a-2db913b12bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358516} {:answer/id #uuid "ec92d1cb-40e5-5ba8-a10b-e0f065531685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358517} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5699c0-de88-537a-9311-86197a3b5ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358518} {:answer/id #uuid "78faa54b-32fd-52b3-a23a-c9b6a516ede1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358519} {:answer/id #uuid "22bbf7fd-2cbe-55e0-9408-f3e829be27e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22bbf7fd-2cbe-55e0-9408-f3e829be27e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358520} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e0a427b-01a7-5e48-85b4-c36e85edbaba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d89bb6ec-58ba-5dce-8d06-89f659d78ac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-49308" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/288b3eeaf1168316f2d8f43fa9484032daa8c9006a1c8355b8edfd1186dc2add.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-4}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(2).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/9f570d7f04bcf5645a97a6a1d85e72f602c436ef184e841fc4c998c0d5eb8e2e.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-4,-1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [-1,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-4,-1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [-1,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (1+2) \\\\  & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+4.5=10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce75730f-0abd-5a62-a8da-af8f643267ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289478} {:answer/id #uuid "b1eeed81-b601-5d36-8662-1b29afc11416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289479} {:answer/id #uuid "bfafa281-9e49-5624-a306-02521053ef2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289480} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3c6b0b-b7cb-5aa7-b48a-fca8bc5707f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289481} {:answer/id #uuid "eae11aa8-42f4-5303-ad12-23a053ac5d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1eeed81-b601-5d36-8662-1b29afc11416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289479} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae626f66-c783-5ca3-aebd-a60b3d9d70b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e894ab8-304a-5125-a424-65cae6e46215" :question/math-academy-id "q-49309" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/af84278943fcf2d9c4e3b99c115a4d82672fe50f2bfed8be7be84648985f80df.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068772/assets/4d1f408db7fcb99092f7e61afc4c0a25501d3349790e54983f481408c58f9951.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi +4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (1+3) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=(2\\pi +4)+4=2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
